{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"SRC_001's claim explicitly restricts refusal to 'log corruption from causes other than budget exhaustion,' deliberately excluding budget-caused corruption. Yet its own first countercondition admits that an inaccurate per-event budget estimate can cause mid-write exhaustion, which IS budget-caused log corruption (a partial or torn event at the tail). This creates a direct contradiction: the claim says resumption is 'always safe as long as the log's append-only identity is intact' and only refuses on non-budget corruption, but budget exhaustion itself can produce a partial write that corrupts the append-only identity—a refusal case the claim's stated conditions omit. The problem asks for the cases in which resumption must stay refused; SRC_001 leaves the most directly relevant refusal case (budget-exhaustion-induced partial write) unaddressed by its main conditions.","target":"76af4028de9cae9e290187509f1f9951d8876ba182cc0c79ba0ceaab6bbfbd49"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"9db96f29aac8122107ffd6d3dade484b16df30ee5fefad9d9aff312e5ffa782d"},{"attack":false,"case":"","target":"e5724ae92bc2bb6f4c17295267a2bd2c16aac1d435ab54fc492aecd09b14a1bc"}]}